64-80a1-f11455ee7906"},{"examples":[{"text":"Dry snuffs are often adulterated with quicklime, and moist snuffs, as rappee, with ammonia, hellebore, pearl-ash, etc.","ref":"1896, Universal Dictionary of the English Language:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[4,10],[59,65]],"id":"f59520fe-7d97-421c-8947-809d5eb22e45"},{"text":"[…] most of the women dipped snuff and of course had a spit-cup in the house.","ref":"1937, Zora Neale Hurston, chapter 5, in Their Eyes Were Watching God, University of Illinois Press, published 1978, page 76:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[29,34]],"id":"9233ea2c-a712-405a-8924-507b217bebc9"}],"senseid":["en:tobacco product for packing a lip"],"links":[["mince","mince"],["dry","dry"],["moisten","moisten"],["pinch","pinch"],["lip","lip"],["tongue","tongue"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"chewing tobacco"},{"word":"dip"}],"categories":["English links with manual fragments","English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"glosses":["Fine-ground or minced tobacco, dry or moistened, intended for use by placing a pinch behind the lip or beneath the tongue."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"27fd9046-6b96-4a0c-9c72-257ca53c60bd"},{"senseid":["en:a snort or sniff of tobacco"],"links":[["snort","snort"],["sniff","sniff"]],"glosses":["A snort or sniff of fine-ground, powdered, or pulverized tobacco."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"1351a0f0-ea06-444b-b289-4548174ebede"},{"senseid":["en:a snort or sniff"],"links":[["brisk","brisk"],["inhaling","inhale"],["snort","snort"]],"glosses":["The act of briskly inhaling by the nose; a sniff, a snort."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"bf709353-9de2-436d-a049-85d0a04b7c67"},{"links":[["Resentment","resentment"],["skepticism","skepticism"],["snuffling","snuffling"],["sniffling","sniffling"]],"glosses":["Resentment or skepticism expressed by quickly drawing air through the nose; snuffling; sniffling."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"00e17671-e617-458e